1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,350
Here's an uncomfortable fact to close this section on.

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,128
Every technique so far protects the number itself.

3
00:00:06,128 --> 00:00:10,794
Not one of them protects the moment a human looks at that number and decides what to

4
00:00:10,794 --> 00:00:11,461
do about it.

5
00:00:11,461 --> 00:00:15,849
You can do everything right, freeze the set, calibrate the rubric, validate the

6
00:00:15,849 --> 00:00:20,405
judge, size the noise, and still, in the room, quietly talk yourself into shipping

7
00:00:20,405 --> 00:00:22,460
something the number told you not to.

8
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:26,793
How does someone who clearly believes in the process end up doing that anyway?

9
00:00:26,793 --> 00:00:28,849
This isn't a new instrument to build.

10
00:00:28,849 --> 00:00:33,293
It's the last discipline: catching yourself in the specific moment it's about to

11
00:00:33,293 --> 00:00:37,848
happen, because there are really only two ways it happens, and both are avoidable.

12
00:00:37,848 --> 00:00:39,348
Nobody sets out to do this.

13
00:00:39,348 --> 00:00:43,681
It happens gradually, under deadline pressure, from people who would tell you,

14
00:00:43,681 --> 00:00:46,070
honestly, that they believe in the process.

15
00:00:46,070 --> 00:00:50,181
That's precisely why it needs a defence built in advance, rather than good

16
00:00:50,181 --> 00:00:51,459
intentions in the room.

17
00:00:52,009 --> 00:00:56,423
There are really only two moves, and you already have the defence for both.

18
00:00:56,423 --> 00:00:59,013
You just haven't pointed it at yourself yet.

19
00:00:59,013 --> 00:01:03,075
The first is editing the instrument after you don't like the reading.

20
00:01:03,075 --> 00:01:07,902
A case fails, and someone notices it's "not really representative", and it quietly

21
00:01:07,902 --> 00:01:09,727
disappears before the next run.

22
00:01:09,727 --> 00:01:14,377
You built a freeze-and-version discipline two lectures ago specifically so this

23
00:01:14,377 --> 00:01:16,437
can't happen without a paper trail.

24
00:01:16,437 --> 00:01:18,497
The second is moving the goalposts.

25
00:01:18,497 --> 00:01:19,910
You said ninety percent.

26
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:22,147
The number comes back at eighty-seven.

27
00:01:22,147 --> 00:01:26,915
And in the room, eighty-seven starts sounding close enough, just this once, given

28
00:01:26,915 --> 00:01:27,680
the deadline.

29
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:28,916
Nobody voted on that.

30
00:01:28,916 --> 00:01:31,683
It just happened, in real time, under pressure.

31
00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:34,647
You will hear a version of this sentence.

32
00:01:34,647 --> 00:01:39,593
Probably from someone reasonable, under real pressure, not trying to deceive anyone.

33
00:01:39,593 --> 00:01:41,419
That's what makes it dangerous.

34
00:01:41,419 --> 00:01:43,244
It doesn't sound like cheating.

35
00:01:43,244 --> 00:01:46,542
It sounds like judgement, flexibility, reading the room.

36
00:01:46,542 --> 00:01:51,429
And it's the exact moment where every hour you spent building a rigorous instrument

37
00:01:51,429 --> 00:01:53,549
gets quietly overruled by a feeling.

38
00:01:53,549 --> 00:01:56,316
The fix isn't a stronger argument in that room.

39
00:01:56,316 --> 00:02:01,263
It's making sure that sentence has nowhere to land, because the decision already got

40
00:02:01,263 --> 00:02:03,559
made somewhere else, earlier, on paper.

41
00:02:03,559 --> 00:02:06,327
Notice, too, that nobody in that room is lying.

42
00:02:06,327 --> 00:02:09,978
Everyone believes what they're saying while they're saying it.

43
00:02:09,978 --> 00:02:14,924
That's what makes a felt-in-the- moment threshold so much weaker than a written one:

44
00:02:14,924 --> 00:02:18,221
it has no memory of what it used to believe an hour ago.

45
00:02:18,771 --> 00:02:22,876
Borrow a word from a field that solved this problem before you did.

46
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:27,838
Clinical trials pre-register their success criteria for exactly this reason: once

47
00:02:27,838 --> 00:02:32,861
you've seen the result, everyone's judgement about what would have counted as good

48
00:02:32,861 --> 00:02:34,515
quietly shifts to match it.

49
00:02:34,515 --> 00:02:36,291
The fix costs almost nothing.

50
00:02:36,291 --> 00:02:40,212
Write the threshold down, in the document, before the eval runs.

51
00:02:40,212 --> 00:02:42,111
Get one other person to see it.

52
00:02:42,111 --> 00:02:47,134
Nothing fancy, no approval workflow, just a timestamp that exists before the score

53
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:47,441
does.

54
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:52,280
Now the debate in the room isn't "is eighty-seven close enough." It's "we wrote

55
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:57,426
ninety, here's why, do we still believe that," which is a completely different, much

56
00:02:57,426 --> 00:02:58,957
more honest conversation.

57
00:02:59,507 --> 00:03:01,820
Three signs, and one is already enough.

58
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:06,681
Someone calling a failing case unrepresentative, only after it failed, not before.

59
00:03:06,681 --> 00:03:11,662
The threshold getting discussed for the first time with the score already visible to

60
00:03:11,662 --> 00:03:12,907
everyone in the room.

61
00:03:12,907 --> 00:03:16,345
And "just this once," said out loud, as the actual reason.

62
00:03:16,345 --> 00:03:18,598
None of these make anyone a bad actor.

63
00:03:18,598 --> 00:03:23,460
They're just what pressure does to judgement, reliably, in every organisation that

64
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:25,001
ships things on deadlines.

65
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:27,729
Naming them in the moment is the entire skill.

66
00:03:27,729 --> 00:03:29,152
You don't need a policy.

67
00:03:29,152 --> 00:03:31,227
You need to recognise the sentence.

68
00:03:31,227 --> 00:03:36,207
The person best placed to say it out loud is often you, precisely because you're the

69
00:03:36,207 --> 00:03:40,950
one who knows where the golden set came from and when the threshold was actually

70
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:41,721
written down.

71
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:43,915
That's not an awkward thing to raise.

72
00:03:43,915 --> 00:03:44,685
It's the job.

73
00:03:45,235 --> 00:03:49,927
Say the cost plainly, because this is where the discipline actually gets tested.

74
00:03:49,927 --> 00:03:54,853
Pre-registering a threshold means sometimes it says no, on a date you didn't want to

75
00:03:54,853 --> 00:03:57,140
hear no. That's not the system failing.

76
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:01,714
Slipping a date because the number said so is the entire point of building the

77
00:04:01,714 --> 00:04:03,239
number in the first place.

78
00:04:03,239 --> 00:04:07,989
A threshold that bends every time it's inconvenient was never a threshold, it was

79
00:04:07,989 --> 00:04:08,634
decoration.

80
00:04:08,634 --> 00:04:13,502
The credibility of the ninety-four percent you report next quarter depends entirely

81
00:04:13,502 --> 00:04:17,666
on everyone remembering that eighty-seven, once, genuinely didn't ship.

82
00:04:17,666 --> 00:04:22,357
That's a harder thing to say in a roadmap review than "we shipped on time." It's

83
00:04:22,357 --> 00:04:27,225
also the sentence that makes every future number you report worth believing without

84
00:04:27,225 --> 00:04:28,984
someone re-checking your work.

85
00:04:29,534 --> 00:04:33,837
One thing before you move on, and it's a five-minute habit, not a project.

86
00:04:33,837 --> 00:04:38,664
Before your next eval run, write the threshold down somewhere with a timestamp, and

87
00:04:38,664 --> 00:04:39,943
show one other person.

88
00:04:39,943 --> 00:04:43,084
That's the whole defence against the goalposts moving.

89
00:04:43,084 --> 00:04:47,736
While you're there, agree in writing what would actually justify removing a case

90
00:04:47,736 --> 00:04:52,621
from your golden set, before you're standing in a room with a failing one on screen.

91
00:04:52,621 --> 00:04:57,447
Next lecture closes out this section properly: what you built, why it was worth the

92
00:04:57,447 --> 00:05:00,995
hours, and where all of it plugs into the rest of the course.
